 intuition:[network('Read the arrows as dependency statements','The arrow means the source requires the target. Follow only the direction the question asks for.',[['P8','Pump'],['P7','Pump'],['S1','Supply']],[[0,1,'DEPENDS_ON'],[1,2,'DEPENDS_ON']],'P7 directly depends on S1. P8 directly depends on P7. The arrow convention must be stated before interpreting a path.')],
 parts:[p('Give each entity a node','P7 is one pump and S1 is one supply. Keep a separate graph record for each of them, because a connection must say which actual piece of equipment it concerns. Each such entity record is called a node.\n\nTwo nodes can share the display name Main unit while retaining different identities. A label groups nodes into categories, such as Asset; a property stores a named value, such as an installation date. A property-graph node can have several labels. Our Python records use kind for one teaching category and id for a stable identifier.\n\nA label groups nodes; it is not the node’s identity. A node can have several labels in a property graph. Our Python records use kind for one teaching category and id for a stable identifier.',null,'Begin with the things the application must distinguish, rather than turning every cell into a node.'),p('Make the relationship a record too','A relationship connects a source node to a target node and has a type such as DEPENDS_ON. Its direction expresses our modelling convention. Properties on that relationship can describe the connection, such as when the dependency became active.\n\nTwo relationships may connect the same nodes for different reasons, or represent separate events. Removing them as duplicates without a domain rule can lose information.',null,'The source and target describe the relationship, not which object is more important.'),withRef(p('Ask a one-hop question before following a chain','For “what does P7 depend on directly?”, find outgoing DEPENDS_ON relationships from P7 and read their target nodes. For “what depends on P7?”, match the incoming direction.\n\nCypher writes node patterns in parentheses and relationship patterns in brackets. The arrow preserves the convention we drew. RETURN chooses the information to display.',null,'A direct neighbour is one relationship away. It is different from every node reachable through a longer chain.'),ref('Read P7’s direct dependencies','This is an external Cypher query over the setup graph.',"MATCH (:Asset {id:$asset})-[:DEPENDS_ON]->(s:Asset)\nRETURN DISTINCT s.id AS dependency\nORDER BY dependency",[basicSetup,'Supply parameter asset as the string P7. Use a read-only database role for the query.'],'S1 is the result. Reversing the arrow would answer a different question.',matchDocs)),p('Represent the same mechanism with ordinary records','The graph dictionary contains a nodes list and an edges list. Each edge names its source and target by ID. We can inspect the relevant edge records without needing a database server.\n\nThis program shows the matching edge identities. The challenge takes the next step and returns distinct dependency IDs instead.',null,'These loops demonstrate the model; database storage and query planning remain separate topics.',[example('Inspect the outgoing edge records','Only the edge whose source is P7 matches.',`edges = ${py(basic.edges)}\nprint([e['id'] for e in edges if e['type']=='DEPENDS_ON' and e['source']=='P7'])`,"['d1']",'The incoming d2 relationship does not describe a resource P7 requires.')]),p('Choose graphs because the relationships matter','A graph representation is useful when questions repeatedly follow connected entities, especially through changing numbers of hops. A simple tabular report may be clearer and cheaper in a relational database.\n\nThe learning task is to preserve identity, direction and relationship meaning. Storage products differ in constraints, transactions and indexing, so a picture of connected circles is not a complete architecture decision.',null,'We will keep the maintenance question stable while adding the machinery needed to answer it reliably.')],
